A rolling update is a deployment strategy in Kubernetes that updates an application without causing downtime. It works by gradually replacing old Pods with new ones, one at a time or in small batches, while ensuring that the application remains available to users throughout the process .

🔄 How It Works
When you trigger an update (for example, by changing the container image), Kubernetes does not stop all old Pods at once. Instead, it follows a controlled process :

Create New Pods: It starts new Pods with the updated configuration on nodes that have available resources.

Wait for Readiness: It waits for the new Pods to be running and ready to serve traffic.

Terminate Old Pods: Once the new Pods are ready, it gradually terminates the old Pods.

If the Deployment is exposed through a Service, traffic is only routed to Pods that are ready. This ensures users never experience an interruption .

⚙️ Key Controls
You can control the pace of the update using two main parameters in the Deployment's strategy :

Parameter	What It Controls	Default
maxUnavailable	The maximum number of Pods that can be unavailable during the update.	25%
maxSurge	The maximum number of extra Pods that can be created above the desired count.	25%
Both values can be set as absolute numbers or percentages.

🛡️ Safety & Rollback
Rolling updates are versioned. If a new version has a problem, you can easily revert to the previous stable version using the kubectl rollout undo command . This makes it a safe method for continuous integration and delivery.

